# Assignment 1.4 — Git Essentials I

## Objective
To convert a local project into a Git repository and learn how to track changes using Git commands.

## Solution

First, I downloaded and installed Git from the official website, https://git-scm.com/. After installing Git, I opened the terminal and verified the installation using the following command:

```bash
git --version
```

The command displayed the installed Git version, confirming that Git was installed successfully.

Next, I opened the terminal in my project folder named `web-development-practice` and initialized a new Git repository using the following command:

```bash
git init
```

After initializing the repository, I configured my Git username and email address using these commands:

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

I replaced the example name and email with my own details.

Then, I created an HTML file named `index.html` inside the project folder and added basic HTML code to it. I used the following commands to check the repository status and track the file:

```bash
git status
git add index.html
git status
```

After staging the file, I created my first commit using the following command:

```bash
git commit -m "Initial commit: Add HTML file"
```

Next, I made some meaningful changes to the HTML file and created a second commit:

```bash
git add index.html
git commit -m "Add heading and paragraph to webpage"
```

Then, I updated the HTML file by adding more content and created a third commit:

```bash
git add index.html
git commit -m "Improve webpage content and structure"
```

Finally, I checked the repository status and viewed the commit history using the following commands:

```bash
git status
git log --oneline
```

The `git status` command showed the current state of the working directory, and the `git log --oneline` command displayed the history of the three commits.

## Result

The local project was successfully converted into a Git repository. Git was installed and configured, an HTML file was created, and changes were tracked using `git add` and `git commit`. Three meaningful commits were created, and the commit history was verified using `git log --oneline`.

## Conclusion

This assignment helped me understand the basics of Git, including repository initialization, user configuration, tracking file changes, creating commits, checking repository status, and viewing commit history.

